@meta
id: 15-04
title: Keep only what matches
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-invert
par: 18
minutes: 4
vscode: filter the output panel by ERROR, copy the results into a new file
open: gateway.log

@brief
The payment gateway log from the incident is sixty lines. The postmortem
wants the errors and nothing else. Keep every `ERROR` line; every other
line, whatever its level, goes.

You could write a `:g` for each level you want rid of, but that is the
wrong way round. The lines you want are the ones that are easy to
describe. What you need is a way to say "every line that does *not*
match, run the command there".

That command exists. It is `:g` with the sense inverted, and it has its
own letter.

@keys
| `:v/pattern/d` | delete every line that does not match `pattern` |
| `:g!/pattern/d` | the same thing, spelled with a bang |

@hints
Describe what you want to keep, not what you want to delete.
--
`:v` is `:g` inverted. `v` as in vim's own mnemonic, "inVerse" — or think
of `grep -v`, which does the same job on the command line.
--
`:v/ERROR/d` and Enter. Everything that is not an error is gone.

@solution
    :v/ERROR/d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes, and a log of sixty lines is a log of eight.

`:v` marks every line that does not match and runs the command on those.
With `d` it is a filter: keep what matches, drop the rest. That is the
overwhelmingly common use, so common that `:v/x/d` should feel like one
word.

`:g!` is the identical command with a different spelling. Use whichever
your fingers prefer, but recognise both when you read them.

The choice between `:g` and `:v` is only ever about which set is easier
to name. Deleting debug lines: the noise is one word, use `:g`. Keeping
errors: the signal is one word, use `:v`. If you find yourself writing
a pattern with three alternatives joined by `\|`, stop and ask whether
the other tool would want a pattern of one.

